<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Nippur</h1><p class="st-meta">2900 BCE – 2334 BCE (approx.) · capital Nippur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The holy city of Enlil, chief god of the Sumerians, whose temple granted legitimacy to any king who claimed to rule Sumer and Akkad.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Nippur was inhabited from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubaid-period">Ubaid period</a> and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a> had become the religious heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>. Lying between north and south, it never produced a dynasty in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a>; instead rulers of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kish">Kish</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/uruk">Uruk</a>, Ur and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lagash">Lagash</a> competed for control of it and recorded gifts to its temple, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekur">E-kur</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nippur was governed by temple officials and local governors, and its authority was religious rather than military: recognition by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> at Nippur confirmed a ruler as overlord of the land. The city was also a centre of learning. Thousands of school tablets from the early second millennium BCE preserve much of the surviving Sumerian literature, including hymns, laments and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> itself.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Its independence, such as it was, ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> around 2334 BCE. Nippur nevertheless remained a major cult and scribal city under later empires, including the Kassite and Persian periods, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murashu-archive">Murashu</a> business archive was written, and it was occupied until around 800 CE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nippur.jpg" alt="Nippur" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Jasmine N. Walthall, U.S. Army · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_from_a_temple_in_Naffur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2900 BCE – 2334 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/30th-century-bce">30th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/24th-century-bce">24th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nippur</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sumerian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sumerian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nibru</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-2617&amp;focus=nippur" aria-label="Nippur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5843 1009 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nippur lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6163 1265L6129 1232M6163 1265L6193 1305"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6129 1232h0M6193 1305h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6163 1265h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6163 1265h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-2617&amp;focus=nippur">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

